 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "In 1985, a huge earthquake hit the Pacific coast of Mexico.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Mexico City was about three hundred fifty kilometers away. It should have been safe.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Instead, the capital was hit hardest. Hundreds of buildings fell.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And here's the strange part. Most of the small houses survived.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Most of the tallest towers survived too.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The ones that fell were mostly in the middle. Around six to fifteen floors.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The reason is under the city. Mexico City is built on an old lake.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Its soft wet clay acts like a bowl of jelly.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The waves from the coast arrived, and the jelly started swinging back and forth.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Slow and strong, about once every two seconds.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now, every building has its own natural swing. Push a swing at the right time, and it goes higher.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Buildings around ten floors swing at about that same speed.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So the ground kept pushing them at exactly the wrong rhythm, again and again.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Today, Mexico City's codes are much stricter, and buildings are designed around that slow swing of the clay.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
